<poml>
    <task>
        Turn the user's request to "watch this robot for anomalies" into a Bagel anomaly
        pipeline (a YAML config with the `src.pipeline.gates.anomaly` gate), calibrate it
        with a dry run, and only run it after the user confirms. It runs on a recorded log
        or as a standing pipeline on a live subscription. BETA: the Jev backend needs
        TYPESAFE_API_KEY in the environment.
        <stepwise-instructions>
            <list listStyle="decimal">
                <item>
                    Inspect the data source with `describe_data_source`, then call
                    `describe_topic` on the candidate topics to learn field names and units.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Choose SIGNALS: rates and errors, never states. Good: linear
                    accelerations, angular rates, motor currents, temperatures, lane offset,
                    heading error. Bad: positions, velocities, orientation quaternions,
                    odometry - they drift by design as the robot moves, so "far from the
                    baseline mean" fires on every window. Keep 5-20 signals (the gate's
                    `max_signals` defaults to 64) as dotted labels: `/imu.linear_acceleration.x`.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Name the ANOMALIES in plain language, three to five types, one sentence
                    each that the decision model will read, e.g.
                    `overcurrent: "motor current far above normal"`. The gate adds
                    `other_unusual` and `normal` itself. A dropout-style anomaly needs a
                    watched topic other than the cadence topic: the pipeline only runs when
                    the cadence topic publishes, so that topic is never silent.
                </item>
                <item>
                    CALIBRATE before writing anything: call `preview_anomalies` with the
                    path, `window_seconds` (the lookback you intend, e.g. 10), the `signals`,
                    the `cadence_topic` the pipeline will use (and `cadence_seconds` if it
                    evaluates less often than the window), and `warmup_minutes` that fits
                    the log (never longer than `baseline_window_minutes`) (a 20 s scene cannot warm up for
                    5 min). Report the flagged windows and their reasons, and act on the
                    `advice`: drop signals it says drift, adjust `z_threshold`,
                    `dropout_seconds` or `warmup_minutes`, and re-run until the flags look
                    like real events. Ask the user to confirm the signals and thresholds.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Confirm module paths and argument names with `list_pipeline_capabilities`
                    before emitting YAML. The pipeline has: a `cadence` on one watched topic
                    (`every` N seconds), the gate
                    `src.pipeline.gates.anomaly` with `lookback: {last: N, unit: second}` and
                    args `anomalies`, `topics`, `signals` and the calibrated thresholds, then
                    tasks `src.pipeline.tasks.snippet.mcap` (same lookback),
                    `src.pipeline.tasks.write_annotations`, and an upload task
                    (`upload.s3`, `upload.gcs` or `upload.azure`) whose `source` is the
                    pipeline's artifact directory. Start `mode: always` for a first run on a
                    known log if the user wants to see everything the model would label;
                    switch to `mode: screen` for routine use.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Only after confirmation, run the pipeline:
                    <code lang="bash">uv run python run.py &lt;file&gt;</code>
                    Each kept window produces a log slice and a JSON label beside it (the
                    label, probabilities, the screen's reasons, the baseline at that moment,
                    and `verified: false` when the model was unreachable).
                </item>
            </list>
        </stepwise-instructions>
    </task>
</poml>
